# Commenting Code
- No obvious comments: Do not write inline comments for self-explanatory code (e.g., // increment i by 1).
- Explain the "why", not the "what": Restrict comments to complex business logic, edge cases, the purpose of the object if not self-documenting, or non-obvious architectural decisions.
- Future-pass AI: Add a comment when it would meaningfully reduce work on a later edit — a non-obvious invariant, which of several similar methods to call, a pitfall not visible from the signature, or a contract that would otherwise have to be rediscovered from callers and history. Do not add comments that only restate the code.
- Preserve existing comments: Do not delete comments if the comments are still correct, if you are unable to determine, prefix them with ?? AI Changed Code after comment ?? -  If you know they are no longer correct, try to update them, if that is not possible, delete them.
- TODO formatting: When leaving unfinished logic or temporary workarounds, format them strictly as # TODO: [explanation] or // TODO:
- Follow the standard commenting conventions for the language (i.e. If in python, refer to PEP sources)